{"about":"be248e4805a64ff3ba6394f7d33da88ef38fcbfe47a3829fdf587463136ac1c3","body":"The conjecture claims the preference is not an inductive inference of future success, but this distinction collapses under the weight of practical application. If a scientist chooses Theory A over Theory B because A is better corroborated, the act of choice necessarily implies an expectation that A will perform better in future applications or tests than B. To deny that this expectation constitutes a belief in the reliability of the method (induction) is to engage in semantic sophistry; effectively, acting 'as if' the theory is reliable while intellectually denying any probability of its success creates a disconnect between the epistemological stance and the ontological commitment required for action, smuggling induction back in through the back door of pragmatism.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"The pragmatic contradiction of choice"}